@title Attributing Gains in Paired Training: Objective or Data Direction?
@kind paper
@source paper:Attributing Gains in Paired Training: Objective or Data Direction?
@cite J. V. Le, Tien Nguyen (2026). Attributing Gains in Paired Training: Objective or Data Direction? Third NeurIPS Workshop on Attributing Model Behavior at Scale: Data Attribution and Provenance (ATTRIB 2026). Non-archival.
@authors Jack V. Le, Tien Nguyen
@note Dialogue is dramatized; coauthors did not say these lines. The Objective and the Data Direction are courtroom personifications of the two words in the title, not a summary of the paper's findings. Figures marked § are quoted from the source.
@status embargo
@verify
@palette night
@cast Jack hue=210 glasses
@cast Objective name="the Objective" hue=350 bun
@cast Direction name="the Data Direction" hue=160 curly
@cast You player hue=20

# Scaffolded by scripts/build-vn-index.mjs --scaffold-paper paired-training on 2026-10-02, then hand-written.
# Embargo skeleton: no paper text is in the repo, so the hook makes no claim about the
# method or the result. ^para chips mark lines that paraphrase the title or the venue
# string as index.html prints them; nothing else is sourced. When the paper is public,
# replace @withheld with @fact lines and the result beat, then set @status draft.

@bg lecture dim
@show Jack left
Jack: Court is in session. The defendant is not a person. It is a training run, and nobody disputes that it got better.
Jack: What is disputed is the credit. The title of the case asks the whole question: objective, or data direction? ^para
@show Objective center (deadpan)
Objective: I am the Objective. I am the loss the run was told to minimize. Every gradient step bowed to me. The gain is mine.
@show Direction right (smile)
Direction: I am the Data Direction. In paired training every example arrives with a partner, and I decide which way the pair points. Keep the loss, turn me around, and the same run learns something else.
Objective (deadpan): Turn *me* off and your pairs are rows in a table.
Direction (smile): Turn me off and your loss has nothing to compare.
Jack: The workshop this case was heard at is named for exactly this: attributing model behavior at scale, data attribution and provenance. Saying which cause a gain belongs to, with evidence rather than eloquence. ^para
@hide Jack
@show You left
@thumb
Jack (smile): You are the jury. Both parties have spoken and no exhibit has been entered. Which is unfair, and also how most people decide.
* Rule for the Objective. The loss is what was optimized. -> verdict_objective
* Rule for the Data Direction. You learn what you are shown. -> verdict_direction
* Refuse to rule until the evidence is in. -> verdict_wait

== verdict_objective
You: The Objective. Nothing moved without it.
Objective (smile): The jury understands optimization.
Direction (worried): The jury has not seen a single pair.
@set verdict = objective
-> evidence

== verdict_direction
You: The Data Direction. The loss only knows what it is fed.
Direction (smile): A juror with taste.
Objective (deadpan): A juror with a hunch.
@set verdict = direction
-> evidence

== verdict_wait
You (thinking): Neither. Show me the ablations.
Objective (puzzled): Ablations.
Direction (worried): Ablations.
Jack (laugh): The one answer neither party rehearsed for.
@set verdict = wait
-> evidence

== evidence
@hide You
@show Jack left
Jack: Verdict noted, and worth exactly what a verdict before evidence is worth. The exhibits stay sealed until the paper is public; when they open, this is where the figures go, each with its section number.
@withheld The evidence is withheld until the paper is public.
@end
